2 GIVN Anna Catharina
2 SURN Bundervoet
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1772
2 PLAC Assenede
1 FAMC @F35@
2 NOTE ASSUMED — this tree draws the link but has not verified it: father: born at Assenede in 1772, where this is the only attested Bundervoet household — but no act names her parents; mother: born at Assenede in 1772, where this is the only attested Bundervoet household — but no act names her parents
1 NOTE A Bundervoet born at **Assenede in 1772**, from the Assenede register index. She is not in
2 CONT the paulderidder list of [[bundervoet_petrus_1727]]'s eight children, and no act read this
2 CONT pass names her parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Her parent links are `asm`, deliberately.** What is known is one identifier and a
2 CONT supporting circumstance: the surname, worth 13 bits in this corpus, and the commune, where
2 CONT this is the only Bundervoet household anywhere attested — the father buried there in 1777,
2 CONT [[bundervoet_coleta_1766]] born there in 1766, [[bundervoet_petronella_1754]] married there
2 CONT in 1781, [[bundervoet_livina_1760]] married there in 1788 and dead there in 1831,
2 CONT [[bundervoet_joanna_1761]] dead there in 1825. The rules want two independent identifiers
2 CONT for a parent link and this has one, so the link is drawn as assumed: the scorer cannot see
2 CONT it in either direction, and it can never come back as evidence licensing another graft.
2 CONT
2 CONT **The chronology is the reason to hedge rather than to graft.** The mother
2 CONT [[dedeckere_marie_1731]] would have been about 41 in 1772, and the paulderidder list of
2 CONT children stops at 1766. That is possible and not at all impossible — but it is exactly the
2 CONT gap where a niece, or the daughter of a brother not yet in the tree, would sit just as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT **What would settle it:** her Assenede baptism act, which names both parents outright.
2 CONT Under objective 2 that is a cheap, well-aimed fetch — the same register run that holds
2 CONT Coleta's 1766 birth.
