1 REFN dekeyser_edouard_1876
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Édouard /Dekeyser/
2 GIVN Édouard
2 SURN Dekeyser
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 NOV 1876
2 PLAC Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen)
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 SEP 1951
2 PLAC Oostende
1 OCCU werkman
1 FAMS @F10@
1 FAMS @F90@
1 FAMC @F89@
1 NOTE Source detail: PRIMARY: Oostende marriage act nr. 81, 9 May 1946 (FamilySearch, Belgium West Flanders Civil Registration 1582-1950 — image read directly). Also Geneanet/AGATHA (children's Oostende acts).
2 CONT
2 CONT WALL BROKEN (July 2026, FamilySearch). Édouard's own 1946 Oostende remarriage act states in full: 'Eduardus De Keyser, werkman, geboren te Hamme den twaalfden November 1876 ... echtgescheiden van Louisa Maria Bocklandt (divorce decree, Rechtbank van eersten aanleg Brugge, ~1923) ... zoon van Desiderius en van Maria Theresia Van den Broeck, beiden overleden.' Every detail matches (werkman; born 12 Nov 1876; first wife Louisa Maria Bocklandt) — so this is unambiguously our Édouard, and: (1) his PARENTS are Desiderius De Keyser × Maria Theresia Van den Broeck; (2) his BIRTHPLACE is Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen) — the SAME town Louise Bocklandt came from (they were fellow Hamme natives who both moved to Oostende); (3) Édouard and Louise did not stay married — they DIVORCED (decree of the Rechtbank van eersten aanleg te Brugge, 14 November 1922 — dated exactly by Louise's own 1924 remarriage act), and Édouard REMARRIED Leontine Schreel (b. Aartrijke 11 Jan 1890, herself divorced from Benoni Joseph Rossel) in Oostende on 9 May 1946. Children with Louise (Oostende civil acts): Jérôme (1897), Gustavus (1899), Bertha Alida Theresia (1901), Maria (1902), + infants Angela Luciana (†1903) and Cesar Mauritius (b.~1904, †1905). This also finally explains 'Hamme' in the old trees: it was the couple's shared birth-town, Hamme (Oost-Vlaanderen) — never Hamme-Merchtem, never a marriage place. NEW FRONTIER: Desiderius De Keyser's and Maria Theresia Van den Broeck's own dates & parents, in the Hamme (O.-Vl.) registers. NAME SPELLING: the family surname is recorded both as 'Dekeyser' (one word — the Oostende line) and 'De Keyser' (two words — his father Desiderius in the 1946 act, and the living generation Roland/Cosette). Same family; each person here keeps the spelling of the record they were found in (historical accuracy over uniformity).
1 SOUR @S73@
2 QUAY 3
0 @I141@ INDI
1 REFN dekeyser_gustaaf
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Gustaaf /Dekeyser/
2 GIVN Gustaaf
2 SURN Dekeyser
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 1920 AND 1929
2 PLAC Oostende
1 FAMS @F91@
1 FAMC @F87@
1 NOTE Source detail: Family memory (Roland’s father). Documentary lead: = Gustavus-Josephus Dekeyser b.1899 (stefpaelinck tree, Oostende), Jerome’s brother — unconfirmed
2 CONT
2 CONT CONFIRMED placement: Cosette confirms Jerome is Roland’s GRANDFATHER, so Gustaaf is Jerome & Léonie’s son — a younger brother of Albert (1920–1972), born ~1920–1928 in Oostende. (This rules out the earlier idea that he was Jerome’s brother Gustavus-Josephus b.1899.) He & Simonne were in England during WWII, where Roland was born in 1943 (plus a daughter). UNCERTAIN: Shaun wondered whether Gustaaf may have died during the war (perhaps in Belgium or France) — but he’s not sure, so this is only a possibility to check, NOT established. He simply isn’t in Belgian civil indexes for a Gustave Dekeyser born ~1920s Oostende, which fits either a wartime death abroad OR a life lived mostly outside Belgium. Open leads to chase next (they’d resolve his fate either way): (1) Roland’s 1943 birth certificate — England & Wales GRO, Edmonton registration district, Q3/Q4 1943, surname De Keyser/Dekeyser, mother’s maiden name Vandewalle (via FreeBMD/GRO); (2) a UK marriage of Gustaaf × Simonne, 1940–43; (3) UK death indexes for a Gustave De Keyser; (4) if he served, Free Belgian Forces / Brigade Piron rolls; (5) Belgian war-dead databases (bel-memorial.org; De Plate Oostende heemkring) — only relevant IF he died in the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHECKED, AND THE CORPUS DOES NOT HAVE HIM (July 2026). His best candidates score 27 bits
2 CONT and are **Josse De Keyser of Aalst, 1809**, **Petrus De Keyser of Aalst, 1840**, and an
2 CONT Alphonse De Keyser at Leuven in 1952. The first two are a century out and in the wrong
2 CONT province; none has a date or place in common with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The score comes from the surname plus a shared forename among children, which for De Keyser
2 CONT in Oost-Vlaanderen is close to no information at all. This is a clean negative: the open
2 CONT Belgian corpus as harvested so far holds nothing that could be Gustaaf, which is consistent
2 CONT with what his record already suspects — that his adult life, and possibly his death, happened
2 CONT outside Belgium.
2 CONT
2 CONT The leads that matter are still the English ones listed above.
1 SOUR @S74@
2 QUAY 2
