2 CONT 1, 24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. She was present and consenting, and like her
2 CONT husband [[bossin_arnoldus]] she signed with a mark, the act stating she could not write.
2 CONT
2 CONT The act gives her no age and no occupation, so this record has neither. The death entry
2 CONT records only that she was alive on the day.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — her marriage to Arnoldus Bossin, and her surname's spelling. *Deyn* as written
2 CONT here is also found as De(y)n, Deijn and Deyns in Brabant registers, and only her own acts
2 CONT will settle which this family used.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (2026-07-26): named as mother of the bride in her daughter
2 CONT [[bossin_anna_1829]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe nr. 9, 1 December 1853 (S22).
2 CONT
2 CONT **And named as nothing more than a name.** Where the 1846 act had her present, consenting
2 CONT and signing with a mark, the 1853 analysis gives her no residence, no trade and no remark,
2 CONT while giving her husband all three. That is a difference worth recording and not worth
2 CONT interpreting: an absent field in a transcription may mean she was absent, or dead, or
2 CONT simply that the field was not filled. Her death bound therefore still rests on 1846, and
2 CONT nothing is inferred from the silence. The register image would settle it.
2 CONT
2 CONT DOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.
2 CONT Nothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the
2 CONT evidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own
2 CONT Demogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or
2 CONT its image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.
