1 REFN peremans_cornelius
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Cornelius /Peremans/
2 GIVN Cornelius
2 SURN Peremans
1 SEX M
1 FAMC @F161@
1 NOTE A second sibling for [[peremans_joanna_1824]]. His marriage banns were published at **Zaventem on 21 April
2 CONT 1844** (S30), naming his parents as [[peremans_egidius]] and *Theresia Verelst* — the short
2 CONT form of [[verelst_joanna]]'s name, which is how half this family's acts write her.
2 CONT
2 CONT The timing is worth noticing. His father had been dead seven years and his mother **four
2 CONT months** — she died on 19 December 1843. He married in the spring after burying her, and his
2 CONT sister [[peremans_joanna_1824]] married in the February after that, at twenty-one and orphaned on both
2 CONT sides.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anchored the same way as [[peremans_maria]]: the record itself gives no place, and the
2 CONT commune comes from the title of the volume the page sits in. Zaventem is where both his parents
2 CONT died and where two of his sisters married.
2 CONT
2 CONT **His wife is not recorded, deliberately.** The index lists three more people in the same
2 CONT document — *Lambertus van der Pelen*, *Maria Catharina van der Vaeren* and *Maria Anna van der
2 CONT Vaeren* — and states the role of none of them. Maria Anna is the likeliest bride and the other
2 CONT two the likeliest parents, but the surnames do not line up in the obvious way, and guessing
2 CONT which of three unlabelled people someone married is exactly the kind of invention this project
2 CONT forbids.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER: the marriage act itself, which would name the bride and both sets of parents; and his
2 CONT own birth act at Zaventem, probably in the 1810s or 1820s.
2 CONT
2 CONT WITNESS LINE, NOT A DATE (2026-07-27): he stands witness, aged 33, at his brother
2 CONT [[peremans_joannes_1820]]'s Zaventem marriage on 21 August 1848 (S34), which would imply a
2 CONT birth around 1814–1815. Not written as a birth date here: a witness line carries only a
2 CONT name and a commune, with no parent names restated, which is *weaker* evidence than the
2 CONT parent-name pair that grafted him into this family in the first place. If this is ever
2 CONT recorded, the only honest form is a `year_span` (`1814..1815`), never a point date.
1 SOUR @S103@
2 QUAY 2
0 @I280@ INDI
1 REFN peremans_egidius
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Egidius /Peremans/
2 GIVN Egidius
2 SURN Peremans
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 MAR 1837
2 PLAC Zaventem
1 FAMS @F161@
1 NOTE Father of the bride in his daughter [[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s marriage act — Zaventem, act nr. 1,
2 CONT 24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. He was dead by then, and the act gives the date
2 CONT and place exactly: Zaventem, 6 March 1837. His wife [[verelst_joanna]] outlived him by six
2 CONT years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The act gives him no age and no occupation. His daughter was 21 in February 1846, which
2 CONT places his own birth loosely in the 1780s or 1790s; nothing narrower is recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — his 1837 Zaventem death act, which would give his age, his occupation and
2 CONT very probably his parents, and his marriage to Joanna Theresia Ver Elst, which the
2 CONT Zaventem registers should hold for roughly 1815–1824. His daughter told the 1846 act that
2 CONT she had never known her grandparents nor where they died, so his parents are not reachable
2 CONT through her.
2 CONT
2 CONT DOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.
2 CONT Nothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the
2 CONT evidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own
2 CONT Demogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or
2 CONT its image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FIFTH CHILD's marriage act names him again (2026-07-27): [[peremans_joannes_1820]]
2 CONT married at Zaventem on 21 August 1848 (S34), and that act gives his own death as
2 CONT **1836-03-06** — the same day and month as the 1837-03-06 held above from S19, one year
2 CONT apart. **Both dates are recorded here, and neither is picked.** 1837-03-06 comes from
2 CONT S19 (his daughter [[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s 1846 marriage act); 1836-03-06 comes from S34 (his son
2 CONT [[peremans_joannes_1820]]'s 1848 marriage act). Both are AGATHA/Demogen index
2 CONT transcriptions of the civil registration — neither is a register image. Positing two
2 CONT separate Egidius Peremans × Joanna Theresia Verelst couples in Zaventem, whose wives
2 CONT happen to share an identical day-level death date (see [[verelst_joanna]]), is a far
2 CONT larger coincidence than a single-digit transcription slip in one of the two independent
2 CONT Demogen batches — so this is read as one man, not two. But which digit slipped is not
2 CONT decided by that reasoning, and the frontmatter `death.date` field is left at 1837-03-06,
2 CONT the earlier-held value, rather than silently overwritten by the later one. Only the
2 CONT Zaventem 1836/1837 death register image — or the act itself — settles which year is
2 CONT correct.
1 SOUR @S104@
