1 REFN peremans_joanna_1824
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Joanna Catharina Jacoba /Peremans/
2 GIVN Joanna Catharina Jacoba
2 SURN Peremans
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1824
2 PLAC Zaventem
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1907
1 OCCU huishoudster (housekeeper)
1 FAMS @F16@
1 FAMC @F161@
1 NOTE CORROBORATED (July 2026) as a mother, by her daughter's Kraainem marriage act of
2 CONT 20 June 1872, which names the bride's parents as Guilielmus Bossin and *Anna* Catharina
2 CONT Peremans. The act's forenames are shorter than the ones this record carries — Anna
2 CONT Catharina against Joanna Catharina Jacoba — which is ordinary for a civil act quoting a
2 CONT parent, but it is a difference, and it is recorded rather than smoothed over.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (July 2026): named in her daughter's 1872 marriage act as *huishoudster*, living
2 CONT at Kraainem. The act calls her Anna Catharina; this record calls her Joanna Catharina
2 CONT Jacoba. Both stand.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (2026-07-26): her own marriage act — Zaventem nr. 1, 24 February 1846 (S19),
2 CONT read as an image. It **settles the forename dispute above in this record's favour**: the
2 CONT act that married her calls her *Joanna Catharina Jacoba Peremans*, in full, which is what
2 CONT this tree held. The shorter *Anna Catharina* of her daughter's 1872 act was the act
2 CONT quoting a parent from memory, and it is now the outlier rather than a rival reading.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was **born at Zaventem** and was **21**, which confirms the 1824 from a civil act and
2 CONT matches her husband year for year. Her occupation at marriage was *arbeidster*.
2 CONT
2 CONT The act names her parents [[peremans_egidius]] and [[verelst_joanna]], both already dead
2 CONT — he at Zaventem on 6 March 1837, she at Zaventem on 19 December 1843 — so she married at
2 CONT twenty-one an orphan on both sides. And it records something registers rarely bother with:
2 CONT she declared, with her witnesses, that **she had never known her grandparents nor where
2 CONT they died**. That is a documented dead end, not a gap to keep searching by name.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — three probable siblings, named and not grafted (2026-07-26). FamilySearch's index
2 CONT of the Brabant civil registration returns, for *Egidius Peremans*, several rows giving his
2 CONT spouse as **Joanna Theresia ver Elst** and a child other than her: **Maria Josephina
2 CONT Peremans**, **Joannes Athanasius Peremans**, and a **Cornelius Peremans** whose mother is given
2 CONT in the shorter form *Theresia Verelst*.
2 CONT
2 CONT The parent pair matches [[peremans_egidius]] and [[verelst_joanna]] exactly, and *Athanasius*
2 CONT is rare enough to be worth something on its own. They are still not grafted, for a specific
2 CONT reason: **none of those rows carries an event date or a place.** Rule 1 wants two independent
2 CONT identifiers with one of them anchored, and a parent pair with no commune and no year is not
2 CONT anchored. The same search, narrowed by parent, then drifted to a quite different family of
2 CONT Peremans in Antwerp — FamilySearch's parent filters are advisory, which this log has recorded
2 CONT before — so the rows need opening one by one before any of them becomes a person here.
2 CONT
2 CONT ONE OPENED (2026-07-26), and it anchored. **Maria Josephina** married *Petrús van der Varent*
2 CONT on **5 November 1840** — and the place, which the indexed record does not carry, comes from the
2 CONT title of the volume the page sits in: ***Zaventem. Huwelijksakten 1840***. Zaventem is where
2 CONT this record's father died, where her mother died, and where she herself was born and married.
2 CONT Place plus an exact parent pair is two identifiers with one anchored, so [[peremans_maria]]
2 CONT is now in the tree as her sister, with a husband and his parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT TWO OPENED (2026-07-26), both anchored the same way. **[[peremans_cornelius]]** had his
2 CONT marriage banns published at **Zaventem on 21 April 1844** (S30) — four months after his mother
2 CONT died and seven years after his father — with the parent pair given in the short form *Egidius
2 CONT Peremans × Theresia Verelst*. His bride is deliberately not recorded: the index lists three
2 CONT more people in that document and states the role of none of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT **[[peremans_joannes_1822]]** is now open too, and his entry is the best of the three: it
2 CONT labels the roles outright — *Vader* Egidius Peremans, *Moeder* Joanna Theresia ver Elst — so
2 CONT nothing is inferred. Born about 1822, he married *Maria Elisabeth Cuypers* at **Zaventem**,
2 CONT banns on 17 and 24 November 1844, registered on the 28th (S31). Her parents come with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT **All three siblings are now in, and together they say something.** Their father died in March
2 CONT 1837 and their mother on 19 December 1843. Cornelius's banns went up in April 1844, Joannes
2 CONT Athanasius's in November 1844, and this record's own marriage followed in February 1846 —
2 CONT three weddings in the twenty-six months after their mother's death, all in the same commune.
2 CONT Whether that is grief, or the family council an orphaned minor needed under the Code, or simply
2 CONT three young people who had been waiting, the acts do not say. The pattern is recorded; the
2 CONT cause is not.
2 CONT
2 CONT **And the 1840 act points at what §47 could not reach.** [[peremans_egidius]] was already dead
2 CONT in November 1840, so marrying his daughter required an extract of his death act, bound into
2 CONT that volume as a *huwelijksbijlage* — the exact mechanism that produced [[S23]]. Vlaams-Brabant
2 CONT indexes no death acts at all, which makes the annexes to this 1840 marriage the best route to
2 CONT his 6 March 1837 act, and they are one volume away.
2 CONT
2 CONT DOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.
2 CONT Nothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the
2 CONT evidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own
2 CONT Demogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or
2 CONT its image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FOURTH CHILD (2026-07-27): [[bossin_joannes_1851]], born Sint-Stevens-Woluwe 15 August
2 CONT 1851, between [[bossin_antonia_1849]] (1849) and [[bossin_ludovica_1853]] (1853) — she is aged 27 on that
2 CONT act, born Zaventem, matching this record's 1824 birth year and birthplace exactly. The
2 CONT act calls her *Anna Catharina Peremans*, the shortened form already reconciled above: her
2 CONT own 1846 marriage act gives the full *Joanna Catharina Jacoba* this record holds, so a
2 CONT later act repeating the shorter form is a clerk's memory of that document, not a rival
2 CONT name needing its own resolution. **That is the precedent this project needs stated
2 CONT plainly, because it recurs**: a shortened form is a settled variant, not an open question,
2 CONT whenever an *independent* document already in hand — not the disputed act itself — gives
2 CONT the full form. It is exactly what distinguishes this case from
2 CONT [[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s: her own record is called plain "Anna" on the act that anchors her in
2 CONT this tree, and "Anna Catharina" appears only inside the disputed acts themselves — so no
2 CONT document independent of the dispute has ever settled it, and grafting on the strength of
2 CONT the disputed act would use it to settle its own admissibility. Here the anchor (S19) and
2 CONT the dispute are different documents; there, they are the same one.
2 CONT
2 CONT She appears again, aged 29, at [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s own birth act (Sint-Stevens-Woluwe
2 CONT nr. 18, 5 October 1853, S33) — again *Anna Catharina Peremans*, born Zaventem, matching
2 CONT her 1824 birth year exactly.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FIFTH SIBLING (2026-07-27): [[peremans_joannes_1820]] married **Joanna Catharina Van
2 CONT Gindertaelen** at Zaventem on **21 August 1848** (S34). This one clears a higher bar than
2 CONT the three found in 2026-07-26: it carries an exact day-level match to their mother's
2 CONT already-held death date (19 December 1843, Zaventem) from a *separately transcribed*
2 CONT register, not merely the parent-name pair with a place borrowed from a volume title. The
2 CONT same act also surfaces a one-year conflict on their father's own death date — 1836 here
2 CONT against 1837 in this record's own S19 — left open, and recorded in full on
2 CONT [[peremans_egidius]] rather than picked here.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FIFTH AND SIXTH CHILD (2026-07-27): [[bossin_henricus_1863]] (b. Kraainem, 1 January 1863)
2 CONT and [[bossin_joannes_1866]] (b. Kraainem, 17 March 1866), from their own 1887
2 CONT Kraainem and 1896 Werchter marriage acts. Both name this record as *Joanna Catharina
2 CONT Peremans*, landbouwster of Kraainem — the fuller form, closer to the S19 anchor's *Joanna
2 CONT Catharina Jacoba* than the *Anna Catharina* several other acts in this family use — again
2 CONT the already-reconciled variant, not a new dispute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of her daughter [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s acts corroborate this record twice more: her own
2 CONT 1884 marriage act (*Anna Catharina Peremans*) and her 1901 remarriage act (*Anna Catharina
2 CONT Peremans*, again zonder beroep) — the shortened form, matching the pattern already
2 CONT documented above.
2 CONT
2 CONT **TWO REGISTER IMAGES READ (2026-07-27), and a THIRD forename variant surfaces.** The
2 CONT Kraainem marriage act and the Alsemberg banns behind [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s 1884 wedding
2 CONT were both read as images, not indexes, and between them this record's mother appears in
2 CONT **three different forms across three documents**: ***Joanna Maria*** on the Alsemberg
2 CONT banns (both act nr. 7 and act nr. 8), ***Anna Catharina*** on the Kraainem marriage act
2 CONT eight days later — where she is **present in person, signs "A.C. Peremans"**, confirmed by
2 CONT direct inspection of the signature line — and ***Joanna Catharina*** on her son
2 CONT [[bossin_henricus_1863]]'s 1887 Kraainem marriage act, where she again signs, this time
2 CONT **"C. Peremans"**. One woman's own signature in two different forms, three years apart, in
2 CONT addition to the *Joanna Catharina Jacoba* of her own 1846 marriage act (S19) and the *Anna
2 CONT Catharina* the family's several other acts already use.
2 CONT
2 CONT **This still holds together as one woman, and §65's ruling is explicitly NOT reopened.**
2 CONT What anchors her identity is not any of the disputed acts themselves but two things
2 CONT independent of all of them: her husband, named identically as **Guilielmus/Guillelmus
2 CONT Bossin** across every one of these documents, and her daughter [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s own
2 CONT **exact day-level birth date and place** (5 October 1853, Sint-Stevens-Woluwe), restated
2 CONT without variation across her birth act, her 1884 marriage act and her 1901 remarriage act.
2 CONT That is precisely what distinguishes this case from [[haesaerts_anna_1810]]'s, where no
2 CONT document independent of the disputed acts ever settled her forename: there, the anchor and
2 CONT the dispute are the same acts; here, the anchor (S19, plus the daughter's birth) sits
2 CONT outside the dispute entirely, so a third spelling variant is one more clerk's rendering of
2 CONT the same well-anchored woman, not a new question to resolve.
1 SOUR @S105@
2 QUAY 3
