1 REFN swaelens_joannes_1858
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Joannes Baptista Julianus /Swaelens/
2 GIVN Joannes Baptista Julianus
2 SURN Swaelens
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 MAY 1858
2 PLAC Alsemberg
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 OCT 1898
2 PLAC Kraainem
1 OCCU schrijnwerker (carpenter)
1 FAMS @F24@
1 FAMC @F1@
1 NOTE Married [[bossin_ludovica_1853]] and, through her, is [[bossin_antonia_1849]]'s brother-in-law. This
2 CONT record previously held nothing beyond that marriage and the bride's parents, from S24's
2 CONT bare index row. It is filled in (2026-07-27) from the civil marriage act itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Own marriage act, Kraainem, 25 February 1884** — a fuller record of the same wedding
2 CONT S24 already carried, not a replacement of it (see the date/place note below). It states
2 CONT his birth directly: **Alsemberg, 11 May 1858**, and his trade, *schrijnwerker*. His
2 CONT parents are named: **Egidius Swaelens**, hovenier of Braine-l'Alleud, and **Joanna
2 CONT Ackermans**, already dead — Alsemberg, 27 December 1877 — see [[swaelens_egidius]] and
2 CONT [[ackermans_joanna]].
2 CONT
2 CONT **Corroborated internally**: standing witness at [[bossin_henricus_1863]]'s wedding — Kraainem,
2 CONT 23 November 1887 — he is given as 29, *schrijnwerker*, resident Kraainem by then. Age 29
2 CONT on that date is an exact arithmetic match to the 1858-05-11 birth this act gives, and
2 CONT shows he had relocated to his wife's commune within three years of marrying.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Death dated (2026-07-27)**: named as [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s deceased "vorige partner
2 CONT bruid" in her 1901 remarriage act — Kraainem, 30 May 1901 — which gives his death as
2 CONT **Kraainem, 7 October 1898**. Two identifiers make this unambiguous rather than a
2 CONT namesake's death: the rare triple-forename-plus-surname combination, and the bride named
2 CONT alongside him in that same act is independently confirmed as this tree's
2 CONT [[bossin_ludovica_1853]] by an exact match on her own birth date and place.
2 CONT
2 CONT **THE REGISTER IMAGES WERE READ (2026-07-27), and both this record and its confidence are
2 CONT upgraded from `sup` to `doc`.** Two things previously left open close.
2 CONT
2 CONT **The 17-vs-25 February discrepancy is SETTLED, not merely reconciled by inference.**
2 CONT Both documents now say it outright, not just plausibly. The Kraainem marriage act states,
2 CONT in its own words, that the banns were published "op de Zondagen tienden en zeventienden
2 CONT Februari laatstleden" — the tenth and seventeenth of February — at Kraainem and Alsemberg.
2 CONT Independently, the **Alsemberg banns register itself** was read: acts nrs. 7 and 8, each
2 CONT printed **AKTE VAN HUWELIJKSAFKONDIGING** (a banns publication, not a marriage), dated
2 CONT Sunday 10 February ("voor de eerste maal afgekondigd") and Sunday 17 February ("voor de
2 CONT tweede maal afgekondigd") 1884. **S24's "17 February 1884, Alsemberg" was never a wrong
2 CONT date — it was the second banns publication, correctly read off the FamilySearch index and
2 CONT then misread here as a marriage date.** The marriage itself is **25 February 1884,
2 CONT Kraainem**, now the value this record and [[bossin_ludovica_1853]]'s carry, in agreement as the
2 CONT data model requires; the two banns dates are recorded here, in prose, rather than
2 CONT discarded, since they are a real and dated event, just not this one.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Franciscus Swaelens is the groom's brother — the act states it, closing a refusal this
2 CONT tree made on the strength of inference alone.** The witness clause reads: *"Franciscus
2 CONT Swaelens, schrijnwerker, oud vier en twintig jaren, wonende te Sint-Genesius Rhode,
2 CONT **broeder des bruidegoms**"* — brother of the groom, in the register's own words, not
2 CONT inferred from the shared surname and shared trade this record had previously (rightly)
2 CONT refused to treat as two identifiers. He is now entered as [[swaelens_franciscus]], son of
2 CONT [[swaelens_egidius]] and [[ackermans_joanna]]. **The methodological point stands
2 CONT regardless of the outcome**: the earlier refusal was the correct call on the evidence
2 CONT then in hand — a trade running in a family is one correlated signal, not two independent
2 CONT ones — and it was overturned not by a better score but by reading the document itself.
2 CONT That is the loop working as designed, not a correction of an error.
2 CONT
2 CONT **A RETRACTION (2026-07-27), against research-log §72.** This record previously stated
2 CONT that "AGATHA holds a scan for this act (`HUBRA_00197982_0`) but is logged out — confirmed
2 CONT `blocked`". That is wrong and is retracted here. The act's AGATHA page was reopened in a
2 CONT live, logged-in session: the login modal is absent (the session is up), but the page's own
2 CONT `modal_URL` field is **empty and hidden**, and there is **no img, Mirador or IIIF
2 CONT reference anywhere in the DOM** — the same structural limit already documented for the
2 CONT GEWVL West-Vlaanderen birth project (research-log §64/§74). The local harvest confirms it
2 CONT independently: `abl.jsonl`'s record for this act carries **no `SourceAvailableScans` field
2 CONT at all**, unlike the 1887 Bossin×Guns act, which does — only a `SourceDigitalOriginal`
2 CONT pointing at the now end-of-life `search.arch.be`. AGATHA does not publish a scan for this
2 CONT act to anyone, logged in or not. This is a `miss` with a stated venue scope, not a
2 CONT `blocked` session error, and the search log is corrected to match. The image that settled
2 CONT this record came from the FamilySearch Kraainem film instead (DGS 004716364, frame
2 CONT `_00532`).
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER: any children of this marriage.
