1 OCCU menuisier (carpenter)
1 FAMS @F132@
1 FAMC @F166@
1 NOTE Third of the four carpenter generations. Civil records give his birth as 24 Jan 1804 (earlier trees said 1805).
2 CONT
2 CONT CORROBORATED (July 2026) as a father, by his son's Kraainem marriage act of 20 June 1872,
2 CONT which names the bridegroom's parents as Georges Thumas and Maria Catharina Joostens — this
2 CONT couple. He appears again in his wife's 1857 death act ([[S9]]) as her surviving husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (July 2026): named as the bridegroom's father in the Kraainem act of 20 June 1872,
2 CONT with [[joostens_marie_1810]] as the mother — the same couple his wife's 1857 death act shows.
2 CONT
2 CONT AGE CONFLICT (July 2026), recorded and left open. His wife's death act of 10 June 1857
2 CONT names him as her surviving husband — *Georges Thumas, menuisier of Grez-Doiceau*, the trade
2 CONT this record already carries, so the identification is not in doubt. But it gives his age as
2 CONT **63**, which puts his birth around 1793–94. This record says **24 January 1804**, which
2 CONT would have made him 53.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten years is too wide to be the usual slippage, and the same act gets his wife's age right —
2 CONT 47, agreeing with her recorded 1810. So one of the two is wrong and it is not obvious which:
2 CONT the 1804 date has never been documented, and the 63 was spoken by whoever reported the death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing changed. FRONTIER: the Grez-Doiceau baptisms for 1793–94 and for 1804, and his own
2 CONT 1864 death act, which would state an age to compare.
2 CONT
2 CONT REFINED (2026-07-26) — **the parent link does decide something after all, and the earlier
2 CONT note here missed it.** It compared only his father's *birth* year, 1772, and concluded that
2 CONT fatherhood at 22 or at 32 is equally ordinary. But [[thumas_georges_1772]] married
2 CONT [[quinart_marie_1780]] on **9 February 1801**. A son born in 1793–94 would arrive *seven years before
2 CONT his parents' marriage*; a son born in January 1804 arrives three years after it, which is
2 CONT unremarkable. That does not prove 1804 — births before a marriage happen, and this couple's
2 CONT marriage date itself rests on Geneanet and is only `sup` — but it moves the weight decidedly
2 CONT onto 1804 and away from the 63.
2 CONT
2 CONT It also matters *whose* age the 1857 act was reporting. The figure it got right, 47, was the
2 CONT **deceased's**: the person the act exists to record, checked against her own papers. The 63
2 CONT was the age of a **surviving spouse**, stated in passing by whoever came to report the death.
2 CONT Those are not equally reliable numbers, and the act getting one right says little about the
2 CONT other.
2 CONT
2 CONT VENUES CLOSED (2026-07-26), so this is not a frontier to re-walk at AGATHA:
2 CONT - **his 1864 death act** — Grez-Doiceau deaths are indexed nearly completely for 1860 (49
2 CONT   acts) and then only in a trickle: about two to four a year for 1861–1874. No Georges Thumas
2 CONT   is among them. All eighteen Thumas appearances in Grez-Doiceau death acts of 1860–1880 are
2 CONT   *Charles Julien Thumas as* **Belanghebbende** — a commune official who turns up in dozens of
2 CONT   acts, and a standing trap for any match made on surname and place alone.
2 CONT - **his 1804 birth act** — AGATHA's Grez-Doiceau birth acts cover **1813 and 1814 only** (57
2 CONT   acts, all of them in those two years). 1804 is not in the index at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is left is the register images themselves — FamilySearch's Brabant-wallon civil
2 CONT registration for 1804, and the parish registers for 1793–94.
2 CONT
2 CONT ACT READ (2026-07-26) — **his own death act, at last, and it was hiding in a marriage
2 CONT register.** An extract from the Grez-Doiceau death registers (S23), read as a register image,
2 CONT states that *le douze janvier mil huit cent soixante quatre* Georges Thumas died at
2 CONT Grez-Doiceau, **veuf de Marie Catherine Joostens**, **fils de Lambert Georges Thumas et de
2 CONT Marie Catherine Quinart, décédés**.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three independent identifiers agree at once, and not one of them is a forename: both parents
2 CONT by name, and the wife by name. That matters here, because Georges Thumas is the commonest
2 CONT name in this commune's registers.
2 CONT
2 CONT **It documents two things this record had been carrying on a member tree's word.** The death
2 CONT date, 12 January 1864, is now from civil registration. And the parent link to
2 CONT [[thumas_georges_1772]] and [[quinart_marie_1780]] — never documented before — is now on an act.
2 CONT
2 CONT **It does not settle the age conflict.** The extract gives no age and no birth date, so the
2 CONT ten years between the recorded 1804 and the *63* of the 1857 act stand exactly where §49 left
2 CONT them.
2 CONT
2 CONT The extract was **délivré sur papier libre pour cause d'indigence constatée** — issued free
2 CONT because the family was certified too poor to pay for the paper.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER: it was drawn up on 28 September 1868 *for a marriage*, which means **a child of
2 CONT this couple married at Grez-Doiceau around then**. This tree knows only [[thumas_georges_1836]], who
2 CONT married at Kraainem in 1872. The 1868 marriage sits in the same volume, near image 221, and
2 CONT should name a sibling.
2 CONT
2 CONT That marriage — [[thumas_georges_1843]] to [[deridder_sophie]], 7 October 1868 — is now
2 CONT identified (S25) but the act itself remains VENUE CLOSED (2026-07-27): Open Archives holds
2 CONT zero Huwelijk or Geboorte acts for Grez-Doiceau at all, only Overlijden, so it cannot be
2 CONT reached by harvest; FamilySearch and AGATHA both came back `blocked` (expired session), not
2 CONT `miss`, this pass. See [[thumas_georges_1843]] and [[deridder_sophie]].
2 CONT
2 CONT CORROBORATED, INDEX-LEVEL (2026-07-27): [[netradyle-grez-naissances]] independently
2 CONT indexes his birth as **25 January 1804** — one day off the 24 January held here, the same
2 CONT order of slippage seen across every date this venue supplies — with father Georges and
2 CONT mother `KINAR Anne Catherine`, agreeing with [[thumas_georges_1772]] and [[quinart_marie_1780]]. A second
2 CONT index compiler, independent of both Geneanet and FamilySearch, now agrees on the year and
2 CONT the day is within the venue's own documented drift.
2 CONT
2 CONT NEGATIVE, SCOPED (2026-07-27) — checked, and it does **not** reopen §49. The same table
2 CONT indexes no Thumas birth at Grez between 1783 and 1797 at all (its earliest rows: 17 Sep
2 CONT 1797, 24 Aug 1799, 10 Apr 1800), which could look like it counts against a 1793/94 birth.
2 CONT It does not: Belgian civil registration in the French-annexed departments only began
2 CONT around 1796-97, exactly where this table's own coverage starts, so a civil-acts
2 CONT dépouillement holding nothing before that date is what the historical boundary predicts,
2 CONT whether or not a 1793/94 birth happened — a birth that early would sit in a parish
2 CONT baptismal register, a different kind of record entirely, not a civil one this venue could
2 CONT ever show. **§49 stays open exactly as it was.**
2 CONT
2 CONT A CONFLICT THAT DOES BEAR ON §49 (2026-07-27), recorded fully on [[thumas_georges_1772]] and
2 CONT [[quinart_marie_1780]] rather than here: his parents' own marriage is independently indexed at
2 CONT netradyle as **2 April 1799**, two years earlier than the 9 February 1801 this record's
2 CONT §49 argument leans on. Under 1799 rather than 1801, the gap between the parents' marriage
2 CONT and a hypothetical 1793/94 birth narrows from roughly seven years to roughly five to six.
2 CONT The direction of the §49 argument is unchanged — 1804 still sits comfortably after the
2 CONT marriage, while 1793/94 would still precede it — only its size narrows by two years.
