2 CONT in May 1882 puts his birth about 1817, which the held `1815` survives but is not confirmed
2 CONT by — so the year stays as it was, and it is worth knowing it comes from a member tree and
2 CONT not from an act.
2 CONT
2 CONT `sup`, not `doc`, and deliberately: the two images prove he existed, married, worked as a
2 CONT mason and was alive at Hamme in 1865 and 1882. Neither gives his birth, his death or his
2 CONT parents, which are the three things this record still asserts on a tree's word. See
2 CONT docs/research-log.md §52 for why that distinction is kept rather than rounded up.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — his death, held as 1898. The 1882 act proves he was alive that May, so the
2 CONT window is 1882–1898 and the register is mapped: Hamme deaths 1894–1900 is FamilySearch
2 CONT DGS **004835775**. His marriage to Ida is the higher-value act, since it would name all
2 CONT four of their parents; on her birthplace it should be sought at **Zelzate** (Huwelijken
