1 FAMS @F161@
1 NOTE Mother of the bride in her daughter [[peremans_joanna_1824]]'s marriage act — Zaventem, act nr. 1,
2 CONT 24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. She was dead by then, at Zaventem on 19 December
2 CONT 1843, six years after her husband [[peremans_egidius]] and barely two years before her
2 CONT daughter married.
2 CONT
2 CONT The act gives her no age and no occupation, so this record has neither.
2 CONT
2 CONT The surname is written *Ver Elst* on the act, in two words. Verelst and Van Elst are the
2 CONT usual Brabant forms, and her own acts will show which the family wrote.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — her 1843 Zaventem death act, which should give her age and her parents. Note
2 CONT that her daughter declared to the 1846 act, with her witnesses, that she had never known
2 CONT her grandparents nor where they died: this generation was not reachable through the
2 CONT family's own memory in 1846, and it will have to come from the registers.
2 CONT
2 CONT DOWNGRADED (2026-07-26) from `doc` to `sup`, in the sweep of docs/research-log.md §52.
2 CONT Nothing here was refuted and no fact changed. What changed is the honest name for the
2 CONT evidence: every act behind this record is an AGATHA act analysis — the Rijksarchief's own
2 CONT Demogen transcription — and AGATHA holds no scan for any of them. `doc` means an act or
2 CONT its image was actually read. None was. The register images are the named next step.
2 CONT
2 CONT CORROBORATED (2026-07-27): a fifth child, [[peremans_joannes_1820]], names her again as his
2 CONT mother in his own 1848 Zaventem marriage act (S34) — dead, at Zaventem, on **19 December
2 CONT 1843**, an exact day-level match to the date held here since S19. The two transcriptions
2 CONT are of two different registers, drawn 2.5 years apart, so an identical day-level date
2 CONT repeated across both is strong corroboration rather than coincidence. This is also what
2 CONT anchors [[peremans_joannes_1820]] as her son: see his own record for the reasoning.
